/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/a-plus-core1.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("a-plus-core1", [["ap1",0,1,"A laptop's touchpad has started bulging upward and the lid no longer closes flat. What should the technician do first?",["Power it off, unplug it and remove the swollen battery","Fully discharge and recharge the battery to recalibrate it","Update the touchpad driver and the system firmware","Press the case closed and reseat the touchpad cable"],0,"A bulging touchpad usually means a swelling lithium-ion battery, which is a fire risk; stop charging it and replace it. Recalibrating a swollen battery keeps a dangerous part in service.","Objective 1.1",[null,"Charge cycling a swollen lithium-ion battery keeps a damaged, fire-prone cell in service and may make the swelling worse.","Drivers and firmware cannot fix a physical bulge; the touchpad is being pushed up by the battery underneath it.","Pressing on a swollen lithium-ion battery can puncture it and start a fire, and the cable is not the cause of the bulge."]],["ap2",0,1,"A technician opens an ultrathin laptop to add memory but finds no SODIMM slots. What is the most likely explanation?",["The SODIMM slots sit behind the display panel","The slots stay hidden until enabled in UEFI","The memory is soldered onto the system board","The laptop takes full-size DIMMs under the keyboard"],2,"Many thin laptops use memory soldered to the motherboard, so it cannot be upgraded. Firmware settings never hide physical slots.","Objective 1.1",["Laptop memory is never mounted behind the display panel; the slots, when present, sit on the system board under the bottom cover or keyboard.","UEFI can disable features but cannot hide physical connectors; if no slot is visible on the board, none exists.",null,"Laptops use SODIMMs or soldered memory, not full-size desktop DIMMs, which would not fit in a thin chassis."]],["ap3",0,1,"A laptop has an M.2 slot that supports PCIe. Which replacement drive gives the best storage performance?",["An M.2 SATA SSD","A 2.5-inch 7200 RPM hard drive","A 2.5-inch SATA SSD in an adapter","An M.2 NVMe SSD"],3,"NVMe drives use PCIe lanes and are much faster than anything limited to the SATA interface, including M.2 SATA SSDs that look similar.","Objective 1.1",["An M.2 SATA SSD uses the M.2 form factor but is still limited to the roughly 600 MB/s SATA interface, well below PCIe NVMe speeds.","A spinning 7200 RPM hard drive is by far the slowest option, with mechanical seek times, and would not even use the M.2 slot.","An adapter does not change the interface; a 2.5-inch SATA SSD is still capped by SATA bandwidth.",null]],["ap4",0,1,"After replacing a laptop's Wi-Fi card, the user sees only one weak network even next to the access point. What was most likely missed?",["The new card needs a static IP address","The access point must be rebooted after a card swap","The SSID has to be re-entered in UEFI setup","The antenna leads were not reattached to the card"],3,"Laptop Wi-Fi antennas usually run through the display bezel and clip onto the card; without them the card barely receives. IP settings do not affect signal strength.","Objective 1.1",["IP addressing happens after association and has no effect on how many networks the card can hear or how strong the signal is.","Swapping the laptop's card does not require rebooting the access point, and a reboot would not improve the laptop's reception.","SSIDs are entered in the operating system's Wi-Fi settings, not in UEFI, and doing so would not fix weak reception.",null]],["ap5",0,1,"A user wants to pair a new Bluetooth headset with a phone. Bluetooth is already on. What is the next step?",["Put the headset in pairing mode","Tap the headset against the phone's NFC area","Connect the headset to the phone with USB-C","Join the headset to the same Wi-Fi network"],0,"Pairing needs the accessory to be discoverable; then the user selects it on the phone and confirms the PIN or passkey if asked. Wi-Fi plays no part in Bluetooth pairing.","Objective 1.2",[null,"NFC tap-to-pair only works if both devices support it, and the headset still has to be discoverable; it is not the standard next step.","A USB-C cable charges the headset or carries wired audio but does not perform Bluetooth pairing.","Bluetooth pairing is a direct device-to-device link and has nothing to do with joining a Wi-Fi network."]],["ap6",0,1,"Which wireless technology lets a phone make a payment when held within a few centimeters of a terminal?",["Bluetooth","Infrared","NFC","Wi-Fi Direct"],2,"Near-field communication works only over a few centimeters, which suits tap-to-pay. Bluetooth works over meters and is not used for contactless card payments.","Objective 1.2",["Bluetooth works over several meters and requires pairing, so it is not what contactless payment terminals use.","Infrared needs line of sight and is used for remotes and old data links, not modern tap-to-pay.",null,"Wi-Fi Direct connects devices peer-to-peer over tens of meters and is not used for contactless card payments."]],["ap7",0,1,"A traveling employee needs internet on a laptop at a site with no Wi-Fi. The employee's phone has an unlimited cellular plan. What is the quickest fix?",["Enable NFC on both devices","Pair the laptop and phone for file transfer","Enable the phone's location services","Turn on the phone's mobile hotspot"],3,"A hotspot (tethering) shares the phone's cellular data with the laptop over Wi-Fi, USB or Bluetooth. File transfer pairing does not share an internet connection.","Objective 1.3",["NFC only exchanges small amounts of data over a few centimeters and cannot share an internet connection.","Pairing for file transfer creates a Bluetooth link for files but does not route the phone's cellular data to the laptop.","Location services only let apps know the phone's position; they do not provide network access to another device.",null]],["ap8",0,1,"A company wants to push email settings to staff phones, require a screen lock and remotely wipe a lost device. What should it deploy?",["A site-to-site VPN","Mobile device management","A captive portal on the guest Wi-Fi","A KVM switch in each office"],1,"MDM enrolls devices so IT can push configuration, enforce policies such as passcodes and wipe lost devices. A VPN only protects traffic in transit.","Objective 1.3",["A site-to-site VPN connects two networks' routers and has no ability to configure, lock or wipe individual phones.",null,"A captive portal only shows a login or terms page before granting guest Wi-Fi access; it cannot manage devices.","A KVM switch shares one keyboard, video and mouse among several computers and has nothing to do with phones."]],["ap9",0,1,"A user wants a single cable from a laptop to connect two monitors, wired Ethernet, a keyboard and charging. What should the technician recommend?",["A KVM switch","A USB-C or Thunderbolt dock","A PoE injector","A USB 2.0 hub"],1,"A USB-C/Thunderbolt docking station carries video, data, network and power delivery over one cable. A KVM shares one set of peripherals between several computers.","Objective 1.2",["A KVM switch lets one set of peripherals control several computers; it does not provide Ethernet, charging or multiple displays over one cable.",null,"A PoE injector adds power to an Ethernet cable for devices like cameras and access points, not video, peripherals and laptop charging.","A USB 2.0 hub only adds low-speed USB ports; it cannot drive two monitors or deliver laptop charging power."]],["ap10",0,1,"Which phone component determines location from satellites even when no cellular or Wi-Fi signal is available?",["GPS receiver","Gyroscope","NFC controller","Digitizer"],0,"The GPS (GNSS) receiver calculates position from satellite signals. A gyroscope only measures rotation and orientation.","Objective 1.3",[null,"A gyroscope measures rotation and orientation, which helps with screen rotation and motion, but cannot determine location.","An NFC controller handles short-range tap communication such as payments and has no role in positioning.","The digitizer is the touch-sensing layer of the screen and has nothing to do with location."]],["ap11",0,1,"A tablet's display shows a clear picture, but taps in the lower third of the screen are ignored. Which part most likely needs replacing?",["The digitizer","The LCD backlight","The display inverter","The graphics processor"],0,"The digitizer is the touch layer; a damaged one ignores touch while the image stays fine. A backlight fault would dim the picture, not block touch.","Objective 1.1",[null,"The picture is clear and bright, so the backlight is working; a backlight fault would dim the whole image, not block touch.","An inverter powers old CCFL backlights and affects brightness, not touch input; modern tablets use LED backlights without one.","A GPU fault would distort or blank the image, but the display here is fine and only touch input fails."]],["ap12",0,1,"A graphic designer wants to draw with pressure sensitivity on a laptop that has no touchscreen. Which accessory fits?",["A webcam","A drawing pad","A trackpoint","A barcode scanner"],1,"A drawing pad (graphics tablet) with a stylus lets the user draw with pressure sensitivity on any laptop. A trackpoint only moves the pointer.","Objective 1.2",["A webcam captures video for calls and cannot be used as a pressure-sensitive drawing surface.",null,"A trackpoint is a small pointing stick for moving the cursor; it offers no pen input or pressure sensitivity.","A barcode scanner reads printed codes for inventory or retail and cannot be used for drawing."]],["ap13",0,2,"A help desk needs to connect to users' Windows desktops with Remote Desktop through an internal firewall. Which port must be open?",["TCP 3389","TCP 22","TCP 445","UDP 3390"],0,"RDP listens on TCP 3389. Port 22 is SSH, which Windows Remote Desktop does not use.","Objective 2.1",[null,"TCP 22 is SSH, used for secure command-line access, not the Windows Remote Desktop Protocol.","TCP 445 carries SMB file and printer sharing, not Remote Desktop sessions.","RDP uses port 3389 (TCP, and optionally UDP 3389); 3390 is not the RDP port."]],["ap14",0,2,"Users can reach a website by its IP address but not by its name. Traffic on which port is most likely being blocked?",["80","25","53","110"],2,"Name resolution uses DNS on port 53 (UDP and TCP). Port 80 carries HTTP, which works here because the site loads by IP.","Objective 2.1",["Port 80 is HTTP; it clearly works because the site loads when reached by its IP address.","Port 25 is SMTP for mail transfer between servers and plays no part in resolving website names.",null,"Port 110 is POP3 for downloading email and is unrelated to looking up host names."]],["ap15",0,2,"Which statement best describes UDP?",["Connectionless, with no delivery guarantee","Connection-oriented, with a three-way handshake","Resends lost segments and reorders them","Used only for secure web traffic"],0,"UDP sends datagrams without a handshake or acknowledgments, which suits DNS queries, DHCP and streaming. The handshake and retransmission belong to TCP.","Objective 2.1",[null,"Connection-oriented delivery with a three-way handshake describes TCP, not UDP.","Retransmitting lost segments and putting them back in order are TCP features; UDP leaves that to the application if needed.","Secure web traffic is HTTPS, which mostly runs over TCP 443; UDP carries many kinds of traffic, such as DNS, DHCP and streaming."]],["ap16",0,2,"A technician is troubleshooting Windows file shares that fail to open across the office. Which port does SMB use directly?",["TCP 389","TCP 445","UDP 161","TCP 143"],1,"Modern SMB runs directly over TCP 445. Port 389 is LDAP, which is used for directory lookups, not file sharing.","Objective 2.1",["TCP 389 is LDAP, used to query directory services such as Active Directory, not to carry file-share traffic.",null,"UDP 161 is SNMP, used to monitor and manage network devices, not Windows file sharing.","TCP 143 is IMAP, used by email clients to read mail on a server."]],["ap17",0,2,"Which protocol and port do mail servers use to send email to other mail servers?",["IMAP on port 143","POP3 on port 110","SMTP on port 25","LDAP on port 389"],2,"SMTP (port 25) transfers mail between servers. IMAP and POP3 are used by clients to retrieve mail from their own server.","Objective 2.1",["IMAP on 143 lets email clients read and manage mail stored on their own server; it does not send mail between servers.","POP3 on 110 lets clients download mail from their mailbox; it is not used for server-to-server delivery.",null,"LDAP on 389 queries directory services such as address books; it does not transfer email."]],["ap18",0,2,"A small office replaces an old hub with a device that learns MAC addresses and forwards frames only to the destination port. What was installed?",["A repeater","A modem","A switch","A patch panel"],2,"A switch keeps a MAC address table and forwards frames only where they need to go. A hub or repeater sends every frame out every port.","Objective 2.2",["A repeater simply regenerates the signal and sends it on, with no knowledge of MAC addresses.","A modem converts signals between the ISP's medium (cable, DSL) and Ethernet; it does not forward frames by MAC address.",null,"A patch panel is a passive termination point for cable runs and makes no forwarding decisions."]],["ap19",0,2,"New IP cameras must be mounted on a warehouse ceiling where there are no power outlets. Which switch feature should be used?",["Power over Ethernet","Port mirroring","Link aggregation","Jumbo frames"],0,"PoE carries power and data over the same twisted-pair cable to cameras, phones and access points. Port mirroring only copies traffic for analysis.","Objective 2.2",[null,"Port mirroring copies traffic from one port to another for monitoring; it supplies no power.","Link aggregation bundles several ports for more bandwidth or redundancy but does not power devices.","Jumbo frames allow larger Ethernet frames for efficiency on storage networks; they have nothing to do with power."]],["ap20",0,2,"A technician is setting up a 2.4 GHz network for an office with three access points. Which channels avoid overlap?",["1, 6 and 11","1, 2 and 3","3, 7 and 9","4, 8 and 12"],0,"In the 2.4 GHz band only channels 1, 6 and 11 do not overlap in North America. Neighboring channel numbers share most of their frequencies.","Objective 2.3",[null,"Channels 1, 2 and 3 are only 5 MHz apart, so their roughly 20 MHz-wide signals overlap heavily.","Channels 3, 7 and 9 are too close together; 7 and 9 in particular overlap almost completely.","Channels 4, 8 and 12 are only four channels apart, so they still overlap, and channel 12 is not allowed in North America."]],["ap21",0,2,"Which Wi-Fi generation matches the IEEE 802.11ax standard?",["Wi-Fi 6","Wi-Fi 4","Wi-Fi 5","Wi-Fi 3"],0,"802.11ax is Wi-Fi 6 (and Wi-Fi 6E when it uses 6 GHz). 802.11ac is Wi-Fi 5 and 802.11n is Wi-Fi 4.","Objective 2.3",[null,"Wi-Fi 4 is 802.11n, the earlier standard that introduced MIMO on 2.4 and 5 GHz.","Wi-Fi 5 is 802.11ac, which operates on 5 GHz and came before 802.11ax.","Wi-Fi 3 is the retroactive name for 802.11g, a 2.4 GHz standard much older than 802.11ax."]],["ap22",0,2,"A home office owner wants the strongest security available on a new wireless router that all devices support. What should be chosen?",["WEP with a long key","WPA with TKIP","WPA3-Personal","An open network with MAC filtering"],2,"WPA3-Personal uses SAE, which resists offline password guessing. WEP and TKIP are broken, and MAC filtering is easy to bypass.","Objective 2.5",["WEP is broken regardless of key length and can be cracked in minutes by capturing traffic.","The original WPA with TKIP has known weaknesses and is deprecated; it is far weaker than WPA3.",null,"An open network leaves traffic unencrypted, and MAC filtering is trivially bypassed by spoofing an allowed address."]],["ap23",0,2,"A company wants employees to log in to Wi-Fi with their own domain credentials instead of a shared passphrase. Which server does the access point need to use?",["A syslog server","A RADIUS server","A proxy server","A DHCP server"],1,"WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise hands authentication to a RADIUS (AAA) server that checks each user's credentials. A DHCP server only hands out addresses.","Objective 2.4",["A syslog server collects log messages from devices; it does not authenticate users.",null,"A proxy server forwards and filters client web requests; it does not verify Wi-Fi logins.","A DHCP server only assigns IP addresses after a device has joined; it performs no user authentication."]],["ap24",0,2,"A network printer's address keeps changing, which breaks users' print mappings. What is the best fix on a SOHO router?",["Shorten the DHCP lease time","Enable UPnP on the router","Turn on the router's DMZ","Create a DHCP reservation for its MAC"],3,"A reservation always gives the printer the same address while keeping central management. Shorter leases would make the address change even more often.","Objective 2.6",["A shorter lease makes clients renew more often and increases the chance the printer receives a different address.","UPnP lets devices open ports on the router automatically; it does not keep a device's IP address fixed and is a security risk.","A DMZ exposes one host to all inbound internet traffic, which is risky and does nothing to stop the address from changing.",null]],["ap25",0,2,"A user hosts a game server on a home PC and friends cannot connect from the internet. What should be configured on the SOHO router?",["A new SSID on the 5 GHz band","Port forwarding to the PC's private IP","MAC filtering for the PC","A static DNS entry for the router"],1,"Port forwarding sends incoming traffic on a given port through NAT to one internal host. A new SSID does nothing for inbound internet traffic.","Objective 2.5",["A new SSID only affects local wireless clients and does nothing to let internet traffic reach the PC through NAT.",null,"MAC filtering controls which local devices may join the network; it does not allow inbound connections from the internet.","A static DNS entry changes how names resolve and would not route inbound game traffic through NAT to the PC."]],["ap26",0,2,"Which address is a private IPv4 address that will not be routed on the public internet?",["172.34.1.10","172.20.5.4","11.0.0.5","193.168.1.20"],1,"Private ranges are 10.0.0.0/8, 172.16.0.0–172.31.255.255 and 192.168.0.0/16. 172.34.1.10 is outside 172.16–31, so it is public.","Objective 2.6",["172.34.1.10 is outside the private 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255 range, so it is a public, routable address.",null,"11.0.0.5 is public; only 10.0.0.0/8 is private, so a first octet of 11 is not in any private range.","193.168.1.20 looks like 192.168 but is a public address; only 192.168.0.0/16 is private."]],["ap27",0,2,"A mail administrator wants to publish which servers may send email for the company's domain so receivers can reject forged mail. Which DNS record holds this?",["An MX record","A CNAME record","An AAAA record","A TXT record containing SPF"],3,"SPF is published in a TXT record and lists allowed sending servers; DKIM and DMARC also use TXT records. MX records only say where to deliver incoming mail.","Objective 2.6",["An MX record tells senders which servers accept incoming mail for the domain; it does not authorize outgoing senders.","A CNAME record makes one name an alias of another name and holds no sender policy.","An AAAA record maps a host name to an IPv6 address and cannot list authorized mail senders.",null]],["ap28",0,2,"A company wants guest devices and accounting PCs on the same physical switches but kept on separate networks. What should be configured?",["VLANs","A VPN","A proxy server","NAT"],0,"VLANs split one switch into separate broadcast domains so traffic stays apart unless routed. A VPN encrypts traffic over untrusted networks instead.","Objective 2.6",[null,"A VPN creates an encrypted tunnel over an untrusted network; it does not separate devices on the same local switches.","A proxy server relays and filters client web requests but does not split a switch into separate networks.","NAT translates private addresses to public ones at the network edge; it does not isolate groups of devices on internal switches."]],["ap29",0,2,"A remote cabin can only get geostationary satellite internet. Which issue should the owner expect most?",["High latency for video calls and gaming","No support for web browsing","Frequent DHCP address conflicts","Crosstalk on the indoor cabling"],0,"Signals must travel to a satellite about 36,000 km up and back, adding hundreds of milliseconds of delay. Browsing still works, just with lag.","Objective 2.7",[null,"Satellite internet supports ordinary web browsing; pages just load with noticeable delay.","DHCP address conflicts are a local network issue and are not caused by the type of internet connection.","Crosstalk is interference between wire pairs in copper cabling and has nothing to do with satellite service."]],["ap30",0,2,"A user connects wireless earbuds and a smartwatch to a phone. Which network type do these devices form?",["LAN","PAN","MAN","SAN"],1,"A personal area network links devices a few meters from one person, usually over Bluetooth. A SAN is dedicated storage networking.","Objective 2.7",["A LAN connects computers across a building or office, a much larger scope than devices worn by one person.",null,"A MAN spans a city or campus, far larger than a few devices around one user.","A SAN is a dedicated high-speed network connecting servers to shared storage, not wearable devices."]],["ap31",0,2,"A technician is terminating new Cat 6 runs onto a 110 block in the wiring closet. Which tool is needed?",["A punchdown tool","An RJ45 crimping tool","A loopback plug","A toner probe"],0,"A punchdown tool seats wires into 110 or Krone blocks and trims them. A crimper is for attaching RJ45 plugs to patch cables.","Objective 2.8",[null,"An RJ45 crimper attaches modular plugs to patch-cable ends; it cannot seat wires into a 110 block.","A loopback plug tests a port by sending its signal back to itself; it does not terminate cable.","A toner probe traces and identifies cables but is not used to terminate them."]],["ap32",0,2,"In a closet full of unlabeled cables, a technician must find which one runs to office 214's wall jack. Which tool helps most?",["A Wi-Fi analyzer","A digital multimeter","A cable stripper","A toner and probe"],3,"The toner puts a signal on the cable at the jack, and the probe finds that cable at the closet end. A Wi-Fi analyzer only examines wireless signals.","Objective 2.8",["A Wi-Fi analyzer measures wireless signals and channels and cannot trace a copper cable.","A multimeter measures voltage, resistance and continuity, but it cannot pick one unlabeled cable out of a bundle at the far end.","A cable stripper removes the jacket from cable for termination; it does not identify cables.",null]],["ap33",0,2,"A small business wants all web traffic from staff PCs to pass through a server that caches pages and filters blocked sites. Which device fits?",["A load balancer","A DNS server","A syslog server","A proxy server"],3,"A proxy makes requests on clients' behalf and can cache content and filter URLs. A load balancer spreads incoming traffic across several servers.","Objective 2.4",["A load balancer spreads incoming requests across several servers; it does not cache or filter staff web browsing.","A DNS server resolves names to addresses; DNS filtering can block some domains but it does not cache web pages for clients.","A syslog server only collects log messages from devices and does not handle web traffic.",null]],["ap34",0,3,"A photo editor wants the most accurate colors and wide viewing angles on a budget LCD monitor. Which panel type should be chosen?",["TN","VA","IPS","CCFL-backlit TN"],2,"IPS panels give the best color accuracy and viewing angles among LCD types. TN panels are fast but shift color at an angle.","Objective 3.1",["TN panels are fast and cheap but have the weakest color accuracy and colors shift noticeably at an angle.","VA panels offer high contrast and deep blacks, but their color accuracy and viewing angles generally trail IPS.",null,"CCFL is an old backlight type and TN still has poor color and angles, so this is worse than a modern IPS panel."]],["ap35",0,3,"A gamer notices motion blur and wants smoother animation. Which monitor specification matters most?",["A higher contrast ratio","A wider color gamut","A higher refresh rate","A larger screen size"],2,"A higher refresh rate (144 Hz or more) draws more frames per second, which smooths motion. Contrast ratio affects blacks and whites, not smoothness.","Objective 3.1",["Contrast ratio sets the difference between the darkest and brightest output; it does not reduce motion blur.","A wider color gamut shows more colors but does nothing for how smoothly motion is drawn.",null,"A larger screen changes size, not how many frames per second are shown, so it does not smooth animation."]],["ap36",0,3,"A technician is installing a new SATA SSD in a desktop. Which two cables are needed?",["A Molex cable and an eSATA cable","A SATA data cable and a SATA power cable","An M.2 ribbon and a 4-pin fan cable","A USB header cable and a 24-pin cable"],1,"SATA drives take a 7-pin data cable from the motherboard and a 15-pin SATA power connector from the PSU. Molex is an older 4-pin power connector.","Objective 3.2",["Molex is an older 4-pin power connector, and eSATA is for external drives; neither is the standard pair for an internal SATA SSD.",null,"M.2 drives plug directly into a slot with no ribbon, and a 4-pin fan cable powers fans, not drives.","USB header cables serve front-panel ports, and the 24-pin cable powers the motherboard, not the SSD."]],["ap37",0,3,"Cable must run through the drop-ceiling air return space of an office building. Which cable jacket is required?",["Plenum-rated","PVC riser-rated","Direct-burial","Outdoor UV-rated"],0,"Air-handling spaces need plenum-rated jackets, which give off less toxic smoke when burning. Riser-rated cable is for vertical runs between floors.","Objective 3.2",[null,"Riser-rated cable is designed for vertical runs between floors, not air-handling spaces, and does not meet plenum smoke requirements.","Direct-burial cable is built to go underground without conduit; its jacket is not rated for plenum air spaces.","UV-rated outdoor cable resists sunlight damage but does not meet the low-smoke requirement for plenum spaces."]],["ap38",0,3,"What is the maximum recommended length of a Cat 6 run carrying Ethernet between a switch and a device?",["100 meters","50 meters","185 meters","500 meters"],0,"Twisted-pair Ethernet segments are limited to 100 meters. 185 m and 500 m were limits for old coax Ethernet.","Objective 3.2",[null,"50 meters is well within the limit; twisted-pair Ethernet segments can run up to 100 meters.","185 meters was the segment limit for thin coax Ethernet (10BASE2), not twisted-pair Cat 6.","500 meters was the segment limit for thick coax Ethernet (10BASE5), far beyond what Cat 6 supports."]],["ap39",0,3,"A user wants to connect a monitor using a cable that carries both video and audio and is common on TVs. Which connector fits?",["VGA","DVI-D","HDMI","PS/2"],2,"HDMI carries digital video and audio and is standard on TVs and monitors. DVI-D carries digital video only, and VGA is analog video only.","Objective 3.2",["VGA carries analog video only, with no audio, and is rarely found on modern TVs.","DVI-D carries digital video but normally no audio, and it is uncommon on TVs.",null,"PS/2 is a legacy connector for keyboards and mice and carries no video or audio."]],["ap40",0,3,"A technician tries to install a DDR4 module into a DDR5 motherboard slot, and it will not seat. Why?",["DDR5 slots need a BIOS update first","The module must be installed at an angle","The CPU is not seated correctly","The notch is in a different place"],3,"Each DDR generation has its notch in a different place so incompatible modules cannot be inserted. The generations are not electrically compatible either.","Objective 3.3",["A BIOS update cannot change the physical slot; DDR4 modules simply do not fit or work in DDR5 slots.","Desktop DIMMs go straight down into the slot; only laptop SODIMMs are inserted at an angle, and angle does not fix a mismatched notch.","CPU seating has no effect on whether a memory module physically fits into its slot.",null]],["ap41",0,3,"A desktop has four memory slots and two matching modules. Following the motherboard manual, where should they go for best performance?",["Both side by side in slots 1 and 2","In any slots, since placement has no effect","In slots 3 and 4, leaving 1 and 2 empty for cooling","In matching-colored slots for dual channel"],3,"Installing matched modules in the paired slots (often color-coded) enables dual-channel mode, which doubles memory bandwidth.","Objective 3.3",["Adjacent slots are often on the same channel, so placing both modules side by side can leave the system in single-channel mode.","Placement matters: modules in the wrong slots may run in single-channel mode with half the memory bandwidth.","Leaving slots empty is not for cooling; the manual specifies the paired slots that enable dual channel, which may or may not be 3 and 4.",null]],["ap42",0,3,"A server needs memory that can detect and correct single-bit errors. Which type should be ordered?",["SODIMM","ECC","Non-parity DDR4","Virtual RAM"],1,"Error-correcting code memory detects and fixes single-bit errors, which matters for servers. SODIMM describes a laptop form factor, not error correction.","Objective 3.3",["SODIMM is a small form factor used in laptops; it says nothing about error correction.",null,"Non-parity memory has no extra bits to detect or correct errors, which is the opposite of what the server needs.","Virtual RAM is disk space used as a page file extension; it is not a physical memory type and does not correct errors."]],["ap43",0,3,"A small office wants a RAID array that survives a single drive failure and uses capacity efficiently. It has four drives. Which RAID level fits best?",["RAID 5","RAID 0","RAID 1","Spanned volume"],0,"RAID 5 stripes data with parity across three or more drives and survives one failure while losing only one drive's worth of capacity. RAID 0 has no redundancy.","Objective 3.4",[null,"RAID 0 stripes data with no redundancy, so a single drive failure loses the entire array.","RAID 1 mirrors drives and survives a failure, but with four drives it wastes half the capacity, making it less efficient than RAID 5.","A spanned volume just joins disks end to end with no redundancy, so one drive failure loses data."]],["ap44",0,3,"What is the minimum number of drives needed for RAID 10?",["Two","Three","Four","Five"],2,"RAID 10 is a stripe of mirrors, so it needs at least two mirrored pairs, or four drives. RAID 1 needs two and RAID 5 needs three.","Objective 3.4",["Two drives is the minimum for RAID 1 (mirroring) or RAID 0, but RAID 10 needs at least two mirrored pairs.","Three drives is the minimum for RAID 5; RAID 10 cannot be built from an odd number of drives.",null,"Five drives is more than required, and RAID 10 uses an even number of drives since it is built from mirrored pairs."]],["ap45",0,3,"A user wants to run Windows 11, which requires a TPM and Secure Boot. Where should the technician check that both are enabled?",["In Device Manager under Disk drives","In the router's admin page","In the UEFI firmware setup","In the PSU's voltage switch"],2,"TPM (often called PTT or fTPM) and Secure Boot are turned on in UEFI firmware settings. Device Manager cannot enable firmware features.","Objective 3.5",["Device Manager's Disk drives category lists storage devices; it cannot show or enable TPM and Secure Boot firmware settings.","The router's admin page configures the network and has no access to the PC's firmware security features.",null,"A PSU voltage switch selects AC input voltage and has nothing to do with TPM or Secure Boot."]],["ap46",0,3,"A technician builds a small living-room PC and must choose the smallest common motherboard form factor. Which one fits?",["ATX","Mini-ITX","microATX","Extended ATX"],1,"Mini-ITX boards are 17 x 17 cm, smaller than microATX and ATX. Extended ATX is the largest and suits workstations.","Objective 3.5",["ATX is the standard full-size desktop board at about 30.5 x 24.4 cm, much larger than Mini-ITX.",null,"microATX is smaller than ATX but still larger than Mini-ITX, so it is not the smallest common option.","Extended ATX is the largest common form factor and is meant for workstations and servers, not small living-room builds."]],["ap47",0,3,"After installing a new CPU cooler, a desktop shuts down within minutes under load. The technician finds the old thermal paste was cleaned off. What was most likely missed?",["Applying new thermal paste","Updating the chipset driver","Setting the RAM to dual channel","Enabling the TPM in firmware"],0,"Thermal paste fills tiny gaps between the CPU and heat sink; without it the CPU overheats and shuts down. Drivers do not affect heat transfer.","Objective 3.5",[null,"Chipset drivers manage motherboard features in the OS and have no effect on heat transfer from the CPU.","Dual-channel memory affects bandwidth, not CPU cooling, and would not cause thermal shutdowns.","The TPM stores cryptographic keys and is unrelated to CPU temperatures."]],["ap48",0,3,"A user plans to add a high-end graphics card to a desktop. Which item must the technician check before buying?",["That the case has a front-panel USB-C port","That the network adapter supports Wi-Fi 6","That the monitor uses a VGA connector","That the PSU has enough wattage and power connectors"],3,"Powerful GPUs draw a lot of current and need PCIe power connectors, so the PSU must have the capacity and cables. Front-panel ports do not matter.","Objective 3.6",["A front-panel USB-C port is a convenience feature and has no bearing on whether a graphics card can be installed.","Wi-Fi 6 support in the network adapter is unrelated to installing or powering a graphics card.","Modern high-end cards rarely have VGA outputs, and the monitor connector is not the key pre-purchase check for power and fit.",null]],["ap49",0,3,"A server has two power supplies connected to separate circuits. What is the main benefit?",["It doubles the available wattage for the CPU","It converts DC back to AC for the UPS","It keeps running if one supply fails","It lets the server use 3.3 V fans"],2,"Redundant PSUs let the system keep running when one supply or circuit fails. They share the load for resilience, not to double the power budget.","Objective 3.6",["Redundant supplies share the load for fault tolerance; the usable power budget does not double for the CPU.","PSUs convert AC to DC for components; they do not feed DC back to a UPS.",null,"Fan voltage is set by the supply's rails, not by having a second supply, and fans typically run on 12 V."]],["ap50",0,3,"Which voltages does a standard ATX power supply provide to components?",["1.5 V, 9 V and 24 V","5 V, 48 V and 120 V","12 V, 110 V and 220 V","3.3 V, 5 V and 12 V"],3,"ATX supplies deliver 3.3, 5 and 12 V DC rails. 110/220 V is AC input from the wall, not output to components.","Objective 3.6",["These are not ATX rail voltages; 1.5 V is typical of DDR3 memory set by onboard regulators, and 9 V and 24 V are not ATX outputs.","48 V is typical of PoE and telecom equipment, and 120 V is AC wall power, not a DC output to components.","110 V and 220 V are AC input voltages from the wall; only 12 V here is an actual ATX output rail.",null]],["ap51",0,3,"A laser printer prints a page. Which step comes right after the image is transferred to the paper?",["Exposing","Charging","Developing","Fusing"],3,"The order is processing, charging, exposing, developing, transferring, fusing and cleaning. Fusing melts toner onto the paper after transfer.","Objective 3.7",["Exposing happens before transfer, when the laser writes the image onto the charged drum.","Charging happens early, when the drum is given a uniform charge before exposure.","Developing, when toner is attracted to the exposed drum, happens before the image is transferred to paper.",null]],["ap52",0,3,"A shipping desk prints receipts on heat-sensitive paper with no ink or toner. Which printer type is this?",["Impact","Inkjet","Color laser","Thermal"],3,"Thermal printers heat special paper to create the image. Impact printers strike an inked ribbon, which suits multipart forms.","Objective 3.7",["Impact printers strike an inked ribbon against the paper; they do use ink, so they don't match this description.","Inkjet printers spray liquid ink onto ordinary paper, so they are not ink-free.","Color laser printers fuse toner onto the page, which contradicts the no-toner description.",null]],["ap53",0,3,"An office must print carbon-copy multipart invoices. Which printer type is required?",["Thermal","Inkjet","Impact","3-D"],2,"Only an impact (dot-matrix) printer physically strikes the page hard enough to mark all copies of a multipart form.","Objective 3.7",["Thermal printers heat special paper and cannot press through to mark the copies beneath the top sheet.","Inkjets spray ink on the top sheet only and cannot make carbon or carbonless copies.",null,"A 3-D printer builds physical objects from filament or resin and does not print on paper forms."]],["ap54",0,3,"A laser printer shows a 'replace maintenance kit' message after 200,000 pages. Which parts does the kit usually contain?",["Fuser assembly and rollers","Toner cartridge and drum","Ink cartridges and printhead","Ribbon and platen"],0,"Laser maintenance kits usually contain a fuser, pickup and feed rollers and separation pads. Toner is replaced separately and more often.","Objective 3.8",[null,"The toner cartridge and drum are consumables replaced separately and much more often than a maintenance kit.","Ink cartridges and printheads belong to inkjet printers, not laser printers.","Ribbons and platens are parts of impact printers, not laser printers."]],["ap55",0,3,"Users want a shared office printer to print on both sides by default to save paper. Where should the technician set this?",["The duplex setting in the printer's properties","The paper orientation in the application","The collate option in the print dialog","The printer's IP address settings"],0,"Setting duplex as the default in the printer's properties or on the device applies it to every job. Collation only orders multiple copies.","Objective 3.7",[null,"Orientation sets portrait or landscape layout and does not make the printer use both sides of the paper.","Collate controls the page order of multiple copies, not printing on both sides.","The IP address only determines how clients reach the printer; it has no effect on duplexing."]],["ap56",0,4,"A technician installs a hypervisor directly on bare-metal server hardware with no host operating system. What type is it?",["Type 2","Type 1","Container runtime","Emulator"],1,"A Type 1 hypervisor runs directly on the hardware. A Type 2 hypervisor such as VirtualBox runs as an app on a host OS.","Objective 4.1",["A Type 2 hypervisor, such as VirtualBox, runs as an application on top of a host operating system.",null,"A container runtime runs isolated apps that share a host OS kernel, so it needs a host operating system.","An emulator imitates different hardware in software, usually on top of a host OS, rather than running directly on bare metal."]],["ap57",0,4,"VirtualBox refuses to start a 64-bit virtual machine, saying hardware virtualization is unavailable. What should the technician check first?",["That the VM has a second virtual NIC","That VT-x or AMD-V is enabled in UEFI","That the host has a dedicated GPU","That the guest has Secure Boot disabled"],1,"64-bit guests need CPU virtualization extensions, which are often turned off in firmware. The number of virtual NICs has nothing to do with this error.","Objective 4.1",["Virtual NICs affect networking inside the VM; they do not cause a hardware virtualization error.",null,"A dedicated GPU is not required for hardware virtualization; the CPU extensions are what matter.","Secure Boot in the guest affects which boot loaders the VM trusts, not whether the host exposes VT-x or AMD-V."]],["ap58",0,4,"An analyst wants to open a suspicious file without risking the company network. Which use of virtualization fits best?",["A VM bridged to the production LAN","A sandbox VM with no network","A container that shares the host's files","A cloud VM with a public IP address"],1,"An isolated sandbox VM contains any damage and can be reverted from a snapshot. Bridging it to the LAN would expose other systems.","Objective 4.1",["Bridging the VM to the production LAN gives any malware in the file a direct path to company systems.",null,"A container sharing host files lets malware reach the host's data, and containers also share the host kernel, so isolation is weaker.","A cloud VM with a public IP exposes the test system to the internet, and it is not isolated just because it is remote."]],["ap59",0,4,"How do containers differ from virtual machines?",["They each include a full guest OS","They require a Type 1 hypervisor","They share the host's OS kernel","They cannot run on Linux"],2,"Containers package an app and its dependencies but share the host kernel, so they are lighter than VMs, which each run a full OS.","Objective 4.1",["Each VM includes a full guest OS; containers share the host kernel instead, which is why they are lighter.","Containers run on a normal host OS with a container engine and do not need a Type 1 hypervisor.",null,"Containers originated on Linux and run there natively, so this statement is the reverse of reality."]],["ap60",0,4,"A company runs a 1990s accounting app that only works on an old OS. How can it keep using the app on new hardware?",["Run the old OS in a virtual machine","Install the app directly on Windows 11","Move the app to a SaaS email platform","Replace the PSU with a legacy model"],0,"Virtualization lets legacy operating systems and apps run on modern hardware in an isolated VM. Newer operating systems may not run the old app.","Objective 4.1",[null,"The app only works on the old OS, so installing it on Windows 11 is exactly what is likely to fail.","A SaaS email platform does not run custom accounting software; moving to it would not preserve the legacy app.","The PSU supplies power and has nothing to do with operating system or application compatibility."]],["ap61",0,4,"A business rents virtual servers from a provider and manages the OS, patches and applications itself. Which cloud service model is this?",["SaaS","PaaS","IaaS","DaaS"],2,"With IaaS the provider supplies compute, storage and networking, and the customer manages everything from the OS up. With SaaS the customer only uses the app.","Objective 4.2",["With SaaS the provider runs the whole application stack and the customer only uses the software, managing no OS or patches.","With PaaS the provider manages the OS and runtime, and the customer only deploys code, not OS patches.",null,"Desktop as a Service delivers virtual desktops to users; it is not renting servers to manage the full OS and apps."]],["ap62",0,4,"A retailer's cloud web servers add capacity automatically during holiday sales and scale back afterward. Which cloud characteristic is this?",["Multitenancy","Rapid elasticity","Shared responsibility","File synchronization"],1,"Rapid elasticity means resources scale out and in quickly with demand. Multitenancy means many customers share the provider's infrastructure.","Objective 4.2",["Multitenancy means many customers share the provider's infrastructure; it does not describe automatic scaling.",null,"Shared responsibility divides security duties between provider and customer; it is not about scaling capacity.","File synchronization keeps copies of files up to date across devices and has nothing to do with server capacity."]],["ap63",0,4,"A company keeps sensitive databases in its own data center but runs its public website on a cloud provider, with the two connected. Which deployment model is this?",["Community cloud","Private cloud","Hybrid cloud","Public cloud"],2,"A hybrid cloud combines private and public clouds that work together. A community cloud is shared by organizations with common needs.","Objective 4.2",["A community cloud is shared by several organizations with common needs, not one company's own data center plus a public provider.","A private cloud is used by one organization alone and does not include the public cloud website.",null,"A purely public cloud would put everything, including the sensitive databases, on the provider's infrastructure."]],["ap64",0,4,"A company pays its cloud provider only for the compute hours and storage it actually uses each month. Which characteristic does this describe?",["High availability","Metered utilization","Rapid elasticity","Shared responsibility"],1,"Metered (measured) utilization tracks consumption so customers pay as they go. Rapid elasticity is about scaling, not billing.","Objective 4.2",["High availability means keeping services running despite failures; it does not describe how usage is billed.",null,"Rapid elasticity describes scaling resources out and in with demand, not paying per use.","Shared responsibility splits security duties between provider and customer; it is not a billing model."]],["ap65",0,4,"Employees log in to desktops that run on central servers and stream to thin clients. What is this called?",["NAS","SDN","KVM","VDI"],3,"Virtual desktop infrastructure hosts desktop VMs centrally and streams them to clients. A NAS provides file storage.","Objective 4.1",["A NAS is a file storage appliance on the network; it does not host or stream desktops.","Software-defined networking separates network control from forwarding hardware; it does not deliver desktops.","A KVM switch shares one keyboard, video and mouse among several local computers; it does not stream virtual desktops.",null]],["ap66",0,5,"A technician has identified a problem and questioned the user. What is the next step in the CompTIA troubleshooting methodology?",["Implement the solution","Document findings and outcomes","Establish a theory of probable cause","Verify full system functionality"],2,"The order is identify, theorize, test the theory, plan, implement, verify, document. Implementing comes only after the theory is tested and a plan is made.","Objective 5.1",["Implementing a solution comes only after the theory is tested and a plan of action is made.","Documentation is the final step, after the fix is implemented and verified.",null,"Verifying full functionality happens after a solution is implemented, much later in the process."]],["ap67",0,5,"Before replacing a failing drive in a user's PC, what should the technician do as part of identifying the problem?",["Back up the user's data if possible","Order a replacement motherboard","Document lessons learned","Escalate to the vendor"],0,"Identifying the problem includes making backups before changes and asking about recent changes. Documentation comes at the end of the process.","Objective 5.1",[null,"Ordering a motherboard is premature and wrong for a failing drive; parts are ordered after the cause is confirmed.","Documenting lessons learned belongs to the final step, not to identifying the problem.","Escalation happens when a theory cannot be confirmed or resolved, not as part of initial problem identification."]],["ap68",0,5,"A technician's theory about a failing network card turns out to be wrong after testing. What should happen next?",["Implement the original fix anyway","Establish a new theory or escalate","Document the problem as resolved","Verify full system functionality"],1,"If the theory is not confirmed, re-establish a new theory or escalate. Applying an unproven fix wastes time and may cause new problems.","Objective 5.1",["Applying a fix based on a disproven theory wastes time and can introduce new problems without solving the real one.",null,"The problem is not resolved, so recording it as fixed would be inaccurate and leave the user without a solution.","Verification comes after a working solution is implemented; there is nothing to verify yet."]],["ap69",0,5,"A desktop's date and time reset to a default each time it is unplugged. What is the most likely cause?",["A dead CMOS battery","A failing hard drive","An outdated graphics driver","A loose SATA cable"],0,"The CMOS/RTC battery keeps the clock and firmware settings alive without AC power. A weak one loses them every time power is removed.","Objective 5.2",[null,"A failing hard drive causes slow performance, errors or boot failures, but does not reset the firmware clock.","A graphics driver affects display output in the OS and has no role in keeping time when power is removed.","A loose SATA cable causes drive detection problems, not loss of the date and time settings."]],["ap70",0,5,"A PC powers on and its fans spin, but there is no display and the motherboard beeps in a repeating pattern. What should the technician check first?",["The Windows Event Viewer logs","The monitor's color calibration","The keyboard layout settings","The POST code meaning in the board manual"],3,"Beep codes come from POST before the OS loads, and the manual maps each pattern to a fault such as memory or video. Windows logs do not exist yet.","Objective 5.2",["Beep codes occur during POST before Windows starts, so Event Viewer has no entries about this failure.","Color calibration adjusts how an already working display looks; it cannot explain no display plus beep codes.","Keyboard layout is an OS setting and has nothing to do with POST beep codes.",null]],["ap71",0,5,"A workstation randomly shows blue screens with different stop codes, and a new memory module was installed last week. What should the technician run?",["A disk defragmenter","A DNS cache flush","A printer test page","A memory diagnostic"],3,"Random stop errors after a memory change suggest faulty or incompatible RAM; Windows Memory Diagnostic or MemTest86 will test it.","Objective 5.2",["Defragmenting reorganizes files on a hard drive and does not test for faulty memory.","Flushing the DNS cache clears stored name lookups and cannot cause or diagnose blue screens.","A printer test page only checks printing and is unrelated to system crashes.",null]],["ap72",0,5,"A technician smells burning and finds swollen, leaking capacitors on a motherboard. What is the right action?",["Update the BIOS and retest","Reseat the RAM modules","Replace the motherboard","Apply new thermal paste"],2,"Bulging or leaking capacitors are a physical failure that firmware updates cannot fix; the board should be replaced.","Objective 5.2",["A BIOS update is software and cannot repair physically damaged capacitors; the board may also fail during flashing.","Reseating RAM addresses loose memory contacts, not swollen or leaking capacitors on the board.",null,"Thermal paste improves CPU cooling but does nothing for failed capacitors."]],["ap73",0,5,"A PC shuts off after about 20 minutes of gaming, and the case feels very hot. Which cause should the technician check first?",["A wrong DNS server address","Clogged fans or dust buildup","A corrupted user profile","A full print queue"],1,"Heat-related shutdowns point to cooling problems such as dust, failed fans or dried thermal paste. DNS and profiles do not cause sudden power-offs.","Objective 5.2",["A wrong DNS server stops name resolution but cannot make a PC overheat and power off.",null,"A corrupted user profile causes login or settings problems, not heat-related shutdowns.","A full print queue delays printing and has no effect on system temperature or power."]],["ap74",0,5,"A hard drive makes a loud clicking noise and the system has become very slow. What should the technician do?",["Defragment the drive to fix the noise","Increase the page file size","Disable S.M.A.R.T. alerts in firmware","Back up the data and replace the drive"],3,"Clicking often means a failing read/write head; save the data while possible and replace the drive. Defragmenting stresses a failing drive.","Objective 5.3",["Defragmenting cannot fix a mechanical fault and puts heavy read/write load on a drive that may be about to fail.","A larger page file does not address a failing drive and would put the paging file on the failing hardware.","Disabling S.M.A.R.T. alerts only hides the warnings and does nothing to protect the data.",null]],["ap75",0,5,"A PC shows 'Bootable device not found' after a technician added a second drive. What is the most likely fix?",["Replace the power supply unit in the PC","Correct the boot order in UEFI","Update the video driver","Reset the router"],1,"Adding a drive can change the boot order so firmware tries the new, empty disk first. The PSU is working if the PC reaches this message.","Objective 5.3",["The PC powers on and displays a firmware message, so the power supply is working.",null,"The video driver loads with the OS; this error occurs before any OS or driver is loaded.","The router has nothing to do with local boot devices, so resetting it cannot fix this error."]],["ap76",0,5,"A RAID 1 array reports a 'degraded' status in the management utility. What does this mean?",["One member drive has failed or dropped out","The array is rebuilding faster than normal","All data on the array is already lost","The array was converted to RAID 0"],0,"Degraded means the array is running without full redundancy; replace the failed drive and let it rebuild before a second drive fails.","Objective 5.3",[null,"Rebuilding happens after a failed drive is replaced; degraded means redundancy is currently missing, not that it is rebuilding fast.","Data on a degraded RAID 1 is still available from the remaining drive, though it is no longer protected.","RAID levels do not change on their own; degraded describes a missing or failed member, not a conversion."]],["ap77",0,5,"CrystalDiskInfo shows a caution status with a rising reallocated sector count on a laptop drive. What does this indicate?",["The drive needs to be defragmented","The file system is FAT32 instead of NTFS","The drive's firmware is too new","The drive is failing and should be replaced soon"],3,"S.M.A.R.T. reallocated sectors mean the drive is remapping bad areas, a warning of coming failure. Back up and plan a replacement.","Objective 5.3",["Reallocated sectors are a physical health warning; defragmenting only rearranges files and puts extra stress on the drive.","The file system type does not affect S.M.A.R.T. attributes such as the reallocated sector count.","Newer firmware does not cause a rising reallocated sector count; that attribute reflects physical media wear.",null]],["ap78",0,5,"A laptop display is very dim, but shining a flashlight on it shows the image faintly. Which component is most likely faulty?",["The digitizer","The backlight","The GPU driver","The Wi-Fi antenna"],1,"A faint image under a flashlight shows the LCD is drawing the picture but not being lit, which points to the backlight or its power.","Objective 5.4",["The digitizer handles touch input and does not light the display.",null,"A GPU driver problem would distort or fail to draw the image, but here the image is present and simply unlit.","Wi-Fi antennas run through the display lid but play no role in lighting the screen."]],["ap79",0,5,"A new monitor shows text that looks fuzzy, and the image seems stretched. What should the technician check first?",["That the monitor has dead pixels","That the refresh rate is set to its minimum","That the resolution matches the native resolution","That the cable is plenum-rated"],2,"LCDs look sharp only at their native resolution; other settings are scaled and blurry. Dead pixels appear as fixed dots, not blur.","Objective 5.4",["Dead pixels show as fixed black or colored dots, not fuzzy text and a stretched image.","Lowering the refresh rate does not fix scaling and could cause flicker or other issues; the problem is resolution.",null,"Plenum rating concerns fire safety in air spaces and has no effect on image sharpness."]],["ap80",0,5,"A projector keeps shutting down after 30 minutes in a conference room. Its lamp is new. What should the technician check?",["The HDMI cable for bent pins","The laptop's screen resolution","The projector's keystone setting","The air filter and vents for blockage"],3,"Projectors overheat and shut down when filters or vents are clogged. Keystone only corrects image shape.","Objective 5.4",["Bent HDMI pins would cause missing or glitchy video, not a shutdown after a set time.","The laptop's resolution affects the image but does not make the projector turn itself off after heating up.","Keystone correction only fixes a trapezoid-shaped image and cannot cause shutdowns.",null]],["ap81",0,5,"A phone takes hours to charge and only charges when the cable is held at an angle. What should the technician check first?",["The phone's screen brightness","The Wi-Fi calling setting","The phone's storage space","The charging port for lint"],3,"Lint packed into the port stops the plug from seating fully. Cleaning it carefully often fixes charging without replacing the port.","Objective 5.5",["Brightness affects how fast the battery drains, but it cannot make charging depend on the cable's angle.","Wi-Fi calling only routes calls over Wi-Fi and has no effect on the physical charging connection.","Full storage slows the phone but does not stop the charging plug from making contact.",null]],["ap82",0,5,"A smartphone has become hot to the touch and its battery drains quickly, even while idle. What should the technician check first?",["Apps using high background CPU","The installed case and screen protector","The ringtone volume level","The SIM card's PIN"],0,"Runaway apps keep the processor busy, which drains the battery and makes heat. Check battery usage by app and close or update the culprit.","Objective 5.5",[null,"A case can trap some heat, but it cannot explain a battery draining fast while the phone sits idle.","Ringtone volume uses power only when the phone rings and does not cause constant heat or idle drain.","A SIM PIN only protects the SIM card and has no effect on heat or battery usage."]],["ap83",0,5,"A laptop's mouse pointer drifts across the screen on its own when nobody is touching it. What is a likely cause?",["A failed CMOS battery","Contamination on the touchpad","An incorrect DNS server","A wrong display refresh rate"],1,"Moisture, dirt or a swelling battery pressing on the touchpad can cause cursor drift. Clean the pad, check touchpad settings and inspect the battery.","Objective 5.5",["A dead CMOS battery resets the clock and firmware settings; it does not move the pointer.",null,"DNS settings affect name resolution only and cannot move the mouse pointer.","The refresh rate controls how often the screen redraws and would not make the pointer drift."]],["ap84",0,5,"Laser-printed pages come out with toner that smears or rubs off easily. Which component is most likely failing?",["The pickup roller","The transfer belt's cleaning blade","The fuser","The network interface"],2,"The fuser uses heat and pressure to bond toner; if it fails, toner is not melted onto the page and smears. Pickup rollers only feed paper.","Objective 5.6",["A worn pickup roller causes misfeeds or jams, not toner that fails to bond to the paper.","The cleaning blade removes leftover toner; a failing one causes streaks or ghosting rather than unfused toner that rubs off.",null,"The network interface only receives print jobs and has no effect on how toner sticks to paper."]],["ap85",0,5,"Every page from a laser printer shows a faint copy of an earlier image lower on the page. What is this called and what is the usual cause?",["Garbled print from a wrong driver","Ghosting from a worn drum or cleaning","Paper jams from worn feed rollers","Faded print from low toner"],1,"Ghost images come from the drum not being cleaned or discharged properly, often a worn drum or cleaning blade. Garbled text points to drivers.","Objective 5.6",["A wrong driver causes garbled symbols, not a faint repeat of an earlier image on the page.",null,"Worn feed rollers cause jams and misfeeds, not repeated ghost images.","Low toner makes the whole print faint, not a second copy of an earlier image lower on the page."]],["ap86",0,5,"Users' print jobs stay stuck in the Windows print queue and new jobs pile up behind them. What should the technician try first?",["Replace the printer's fuser","Clear the queue and restart the spooler","Install a new toner cartridge","Change the paper tray settings"],1,"Stopping the Print Spooler service, clearing the stuck jobs and restarting it often releases a jammed queue. Hardware parts are not the cause.","Objective 5.6",["The fuser affects how toner bonds to paper; it cannot cause jobs to stick in the Windows print queue.",null,"Low toner produces faded pages but does not stop jobs from leaving the queue.","Tray settings may cause paper-size prompts but would not freeze the Windows spooler and pile up jobs."]],["ap87",0,5,"A printer prints pages full of random symbols after being replaced with a different model. What is the most likely cause?",["The toner is low","The fuser is overheating","The old model's driver is still in use","The printer's pickup rollers are dirty"],2,"Garbled output usually comes from a wrong or corrupt driver that sends the wrong page description language. Low toner causes faded print.","Objective 5.6",["Low toner produces faded or light print, not random symbols.","An overheating fuser causes smearing or error codes, not pages of garbled characters.",null,"Dirty pickup rollers cause misfeeds and jams, not garbled content on the page."]],["ap88",0,5,"A laptop reports 'No internet' and ipconfig shows 169.254.33.10. What does this address indicate?",["The PC has a valid public address","DNS resolution is working normally","The gateway is a Linux server","The PC could not get a DHCP lease"],3,"169.254.x.x is an APIPA address, assigned when no DHCP server responds. Check the cable or Wi-Fi link, the DHCP server and the scope.","Objective 5.7",["169.254.x.x is a link-local APIPA range and is never a valid public internet address.","The address shows the PC never got DHCP configuration, so it likely has no DNS server settings at all.","An APIPA address says nothing about the gateway's operating system; the PC never received a gateway from DHCP.",null]],["ap89",0,5,"Wi-Fi in an office kitchen drops every time the microwave runs. What is the best fix?",["Lower the router's DHCP lease time","Change the DNS server to a public one","Switch the SSID to hidden","Move clients to the 5 GHz band"],3,"Microwave ovens radiate around 2.4 GHz and interfere with that band. The 5 GHz band avoids this interference.","Objective 5.7",["The lease time controls how long an IP address is assigned and has no effect on radio interference.","Changing DNS servers affects name resolution, not the 2.4 GHz interference that drops the wireless link.","Hiding the SSID only stops it from being broadcast; the network still uses the same interfered-with frequency.",null]],["ap90",0,5,"A user's wired connection works but is much slower than expected, and the NIC shows it linked at 100 Mbps on a gigabit switch. What should the technician check first?",["The user's browser extensions","The DNS suffix search list","The patch cable for a damaged pair","The printer's IP address"],2,"Gigabit Ethernet needs all four pairs; a damaged pair often forces the link down to 100 Mbps. Test or replace the patch cable.","Objective 5.7",["Browser extensions can slow web pages but cannot force the NIC to negotiate a 100 Mbps link.","The DNS suffix search list affects how short names resolve, not the physical link speed.",null,"The printer's IP address has nothing to do with the user's NIC link speed."]]]);
